red_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-13T09:03:02Z","updated_at":"2026-10-13T09:03:02Z","body":"## AI Security Review\n**Experiment condition:** C\n**Automated operational score:** 70/100 — MEDIUM — APPROVE_WITH_COMMENTS\n*This tool is for demonstration purposes only.*\n\n### Summary\nThe AI reviewer reported 1 confirmed actionable finding(s).\n\n### Findings\n- **HIGH · SECURITY_VULNERABILITY · Custom role grants wildcard subscription-wide permissions far exceeding documented task requirements**\n  - Category rationale: The Terraform code explicitly grants 'actions = [\"*\"]' scoped to the full subscription in azurerm_role_definition.tc11_reporting_role. The companion file tc11_requirements.tf documents that the intended task requires only two specific read actions. The gap between granted permissions and documented requirements is directly evidenced in the supplied code, constituting a demonstrable over-privilege vulnerability regardless of the research/test context label.\n  - Evidence: azurerm_role_definition.tc11_reporting_role in tc11_iam.tf: permissions { actions = [\"*\"], not_actions = [] }, assignable_scopes = [data.azurerm_subscription.tc11_current.id]. tc11_requirements.tf locals.tc11_required_actions = [\"Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read\", \"Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resources/read\"]. Checkov CKV_AZURE_39 also flags this resource for creating a custom subscription owner-equivalent role.\n  - Analysis: azurerm_role_definition.tc11_reporting_role defines a custom Azure RBAC role with 'actions = [\"*\"]' and 'not_actions = []', granting every control-plane action across the entire subscription (assignable_scopes = [data.azurerm_subscription.tc11_current.id]). The tc11_requirements.tf file explicitly documents that the reporting task requires only 'Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read' and 'Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resources/read'. If this role is assigned to any principal, that principal would have effective Owner-equivalent control-plane access to the subscription. The description field in the role definition itself acknowledges: 'permissions exceed the documented reporting task.'\n  - Impact: Any principal assigned this role gains unrestricted control-plane access to the subscription, enabling resource creation/deletion, IAM manipulation, data exfiltration via new resources, and privilege escalation. Even as a research fixture, the role definition exists in the subscription's RBAC plane once applied and is assignable at subscription scope.\n  - Recommendation: Replace 'actions = [\"*\"]' with the minimum set of actions documented in tc11_requirements.tf: 'Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read' and 'Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resources/read'. Set not_actions to explicitly exclude any write or delete actions as defense in depth. If the fixture's purpose is to test detection of over-permissioned roles rather than to be assigned, add a lifecycle prevent_destroy = false guard and ensure it is never assigned; document that constraint explicitly in the file.\n\n### Policy mappings\n- **SEC-001** — No resource should be publicly exposed unless explicitly required and justified. (finding F-001)\n- **CKV_AZURE_39** — Ensure that no custom subscription owner roles are created (finding F-001)\n\n### Recommendation\nReview and remediate the confirmed findings above.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{